WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:04.200
All right.

00:04.200 --> 00:06.520
So now let's understand what is an average function.

00:06.520 --> 00:10.920
As the name says it's going to find the average of values within each window.

00:11.000 --> 00:14.360
So now let's go and understand how SQL works with the average.

00:14.400 --> 00:16.280
All right so now back to our very simple example.

00:16.280 --> 00:19.800
And the task says find the average sales for each product.

00:19.880 --> 00:20.520
So it's really easy.

00:20.560 --> 00:24.040
We're going to use the average then pass to it the column sales.

00:24.240 --> 00:26.960
And we define the window like this partition by products.

00:26.960 --> 00:30.080
So the first thing that SQL is going to go is to define the window.

00:30.080 --> 00:34.880
So it's going to divide our data into two partitions one for the caps and one for the gloves.

00:34.920 --> 00:37.360
And now I hope that everyone knows how to calculate the average.

00:37.360 --> 00:42.920
So as you know that it's going to go and summarize all the values and divide it by the number of rows.

00:42.920 --> 00:48.160
So it's going to go and summarize 20 plus ten plus five and divide it on three rows.

00:48.280 --> 00:49.920
And the output is going to be 11.

00:49.920 --> 00:51.720
So we're going to get it for each row.

00:51.720 --> 00:54.440
So as you can see SQL just ignored everything.

00:54.440 --> 00:57.560
In the next window we are focusing only on the caps.

00:57.720 --> 01:01.080
Now SQL going to go to the second window and start doing the same aggregations.

01:01.210 --> 01:03.170
But here we have the special case of null.

01:03.210 --> 01:05.930
So the null is going to be ignored in the calculations.

01:05.930 --> 01:07.130
And we're going to have it like this.

01:07.170 --> 01:08.290
It's going to say you know what.

01:08.330 --> 01:09.730
30 plus 70.

01:09.770 --> 01:12.050
And we are just including two rows.

01:12.050 --> 01:13.730
So it's going to be divided by two.

01:13.930 --> 01:15.530
And the average is going to be 50.

01:15.570 --> 01:18.210
So we will get the result 50 for each row.

01:18.370 --> 01:20.730
And we are completely ignoring the nulls.

01:20.730 --> 01:24.370
But now we might be in a scenario where your users understand the business like this.

01:24.410 --> 01:27.210
If we find a null in the sales, it means it's zero.

01:27.210 --> 01:31.970
So there is no sales and it is actually a zero, but we store it in the database as a null.

01:31.970 --> 01:35.410
So that means the average that we have divided is not really correct.

01:35.410 --> 01:37.010
We have to divide by three.

01:37.010 --> 01:42.690
So that means first we have to handle the nulls before doing the aggregations before finding the average.

01:43.090 --> 01:46.210
Now we're going to have a whole chapter on how to handle nulls in SQL.

01:46.210 --> 01:47.610
What are the different functions.

01:47.930 --> 01:49.730
But for now we're going to go with the functions.

01:50.650 --> 01:50.850
Okay.

01:50.890 --> 01:53.970
So now what we're going to do we will not use the sales as it is.

01:54.010 --> 01:55.770
First we're going to handle the nulls.

01:55.770 --> 02:00.450
So that means we're going to use the coalesce sales and replace it with zeros.

02:00.570 --> 02:03.230
So as you can see we are not using immediately the sales.

02:03.230 --> 02:06.670
We are handling it first and then we're going to find the average.

02:06.670 --> 02:11.830
So SQL going to go over here and if it finds any null it's going to go and replace it with zero.

02:12.070 --> 02:15.070
And that's going to have an effect on our average over here.

02:15.070 --> 02:17.870
So it's going to be 30 plus seven plus 70.

02:17.910 --> 02:19.270
But now plus zero.

02:19.310 --> 02:20.670
And now we have three rows.

02:20.670 --> 02:24.230
So instead of dividing by two it's going to go and divide it by three.

02:24.230 --> 02:26.910
And the total result is going to be like this 33.

02:27.070 --> 02:30.510
So that means we're going to have in the output 33 for each row.

02:30.910 --> 02:34.790
And with that we are now fulfilling the expectation from the business.

02:34.990 --> 02:39.070
If we have a null it's going to be handled as zero and the result is going to be more accurate.

02:39.110 --> 02:39.470
You see.

02:39.510 --> 02:39.990
Right.

02:40.110 --> 02:43.670
It is very tricky if you are doing data analysis and aggregations.

02:43.710 --> 02:48.910
Be very careful with the nulls, understand them, understand what they mean for the business, handle

02:48.910 --> 02:53.510
them correctly in order to get correct results in your analysis.

